# Capacitor's generated Android project keeps build output and local SDK paths
# in-tree. The project itself is committed — it carries the manifest, the icons
# and the Gradle config — but nothing Gradle regenerates.
mobile/android/build/
mobile/android/app/build/
mobile/android/.gradle/
mobile/android/local.properties
mobile/android/app/src/main/assets/public/
mobile/android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.config.json
mobile/android/app/src/main/assets/capacitor.plugins.json

# Personal deploy target: host, account and key path. Per-machine, never shared.

# Build-time OAuth client for sync. See .env.example.
.env
dist-release/

# ── Not published ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These exist, they are maintained, and they are deliberately not in the
# public repository. They stay on the machines that use them.
#
# The whole of scripts/ — the build, deploy, release, provisioning and
# verification tooling. Some of it drives an SSH install onto a named machine
# and publishes releases; the rest is a development harness that only makes
# sense next to the hardware it drives. Note the consequence: `npm run apk`
# points at scripts/build-apk.sh, so a fresh clone cannot build the APK
# without these files. That is the intended trade.
scripts/

# The three long-form design documents. The reasoning in them is the
# project's, not the reader's.
docs/ARCHITECTURE.md
docs/CYCLE-2.md
docs/SYNC.md
